#!/bin/sh
# ventulus-superio-guard — refuse a Nuvoton/Winbond Super-I/O probe on a
# Gigabyte board, whose Super-I/O is ITE.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS (measured 2026-09-05, X870E AORUS MASTER, DEC-332)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
# nct6775 and w83627ehf share one `superio_enter()`:
#
#     outb(0x87, ioreg); outb(0x87, ioreg);      /* UNCONDITIONAL */
#
# and only then read the device ID, on both 0x2E and 0x4E. On a Gigabyte board
# whose secondary Super-I/O sits behind an ITE eSPI-to-LPC bridge, that write
# puts the BRIDGE into configuration mode. The bridge then answers 0x8883 in
# place of the chip behind it, so it87 cannot find the secondary at all.
#
# Measured cost on this board: 3 of 8 fan headers and 3 of 9 temperatures
# disappear. The probe FAILS ("could not insert 'nct6775': No such device") and
# does the damage anyway -- the write happens before the -ENODEV.
#
# On that board the latch survived a warm reboot and a soft power-off, and only
# a full power cut cleared it: the bridge stays on standby power. The recovery,
# as the Hardware Troubleshooting guide gives it, is a ladder -- stop whatever
# writes the unlock, reboot, and if the chip is still missing, power down at the
# wall.
#
# WHY SUPPRESSION IS FREE HERE (DEC-424)
# --------------------------------------
# Gigabyte boards use ITE Super-I/O chips: 131 of 131 Gigabyte models sampled on
# linux-hardware.org, and no Gigabyte board with a Nuvoton or Winbond Super-I/O is
# known (DEC-421, register row BRD-n). nct6775 and w83627ehf drive Nuvoton and
# Winbond silicon, so on a Gigabyte board they can only ever fail -- there is no
# functionality to lose, only a destructive probe to avoid. So the guard
# suppresses them on EVERY board whose DMI vendor is Gigabyte. Until DEC-424 it
# did so only on the boards listed below, which left every unlisted Gigabyte
# board (an AM5 board behind the same bridge, say) probed at every boot.
#
# The list stays, for two jobs: it names a known board in the journal line, and
# it is the ONLY evidence used when the firmware reports no board vendor at all
# -- a listed board name is Gigabyte's, so that board is suppressed too. On
# every OTHER vendor the real modprobe runs untouched, because nct6775 is
# genuinely required on most Nuvoton boards. Gigabyte's server brand reports
# "Giga Computing", which does not match, and is left alone.
#
# WHY `install` AND NOT `blacklist`
# ---------------------------------
# systemd-modules-load issues an explicit `modprobe <name>`, which IGNORES
# blacklist and HONOURS install. blacklist would silently do nothing here.
#
# To override, create a file with the SAME NAME in /etc/modprobe.d/:
#     /etc/modprobe.d/ventulus-superio.conf
# A same-named file in a higher-priority directory masks this one entirely, which
# is the ONLY override modprobe.d(5) guarantees. A DIFFERENTLY-named file in
# /etc/modprobe.d does NOT reliably win: all config files across all directories
# are parsed in lexicographic order by basename, and for a duplicated `install`
# the FIRST one parsed takes effect. Measured with kmod 34.2 -- `10-a.conf` beat
# `90-b.conf` in both orderings -- so an override named e.g. `zz-nct6775.conf`
# would sort after this file and be silently ignored.
#
# FAIL-OPEN BY DESIGN
# -------------------
# Anything unexpected -- an unreadable board name, missing modprobe, no
# arguments -- falls through to loading the module. A guard that breaks must not
# cost a user their fan control on hardware this was never about. (A missing
# VENDOR with a readable, listed board name is not "unexpected": the name alone
# identifies the board. That is the one case DEC-424 decided the other way, by
# the user's choice.)
#
# NO CHIP, OR NO MODULE, IS NOT A FAILURE (PKG-i)
# -----------------------------------------------
# On most boards that are not Gigabyte's, one of the two modules finds no chip:
# nct6775 on an ITE or Fintek board, and w83627ehf almost everywhere, because it
# matches only old Winbond parts. The kernel answers ENODEV, and modprobe says
# "could not insert '<module>': No such device" and exits 1. And some kernels do
# not ship the module at all (Fedora 45's kernel-modules-core has no nct6775).
# Without this rule, systemd-modules-load forgives both: ENODEV, and a module
# that is not installed. With it, they cost something. libkmod reports ANY
# non-zero exit of an install command as EINVAL, whatever the command meant
# (kmod's command_do), and the rule makes even an absent module look present.
# So systemd-modules-load failed at every boot and the system read "degraded".
# Measured 2026-10-26 on Debian 13 and Fedora 45. An exit code cannot carry
# ENODEV through libkmod, so the guard settles both here and exits 0: when
# modprobe reports "No such device", and when modprobe fails and `modinfo` cannot
# find the module for this kernel. It still passes modprobe's message on
# unchanged, so a person running `modprobe nct6775` still sees it, and it writes
# one journal line. Every other failure keeps modprobe's own non-zero status: a
# bad option, a rejected signature or a resource conflict.
#
# Where logger cannot reach /dev/log -- Fedora's SELinux policy denies it to
# systemd-modules-load -- a journal line goes to stderr instead, which
# systemd-modules-load passes to the journal under its own unit.

set -u

# Never inherit PATH from the caller: this runs as root, early, from modprobe.
PATH=/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
export PATH

# Pin the locale too: the board match case-folds with `tr`, and a locale where
# that fold does not map ASCII identically would make the match MISS -- which
# fails open, i.e. it loads the module on an affected board. That is the
# destructive direction, so it is worth one line to remove.
LC_ALL=C
export LC_ALL

# Three test seams, none of which can widen what this script is able to do:
#   VENTULUS_DMI_DIR   -- where to read DMI from (default: the real sysfs)
#   VENTULUS_GUARD_DRY_RUN -- print the decision instead of running modprobe
#   VENTULUS_GUARD_SIM_RC  -- run no modprobe; act as if it had exited with this
#       status after printing VENTULUS_GUARD_SIM_STDERR, and as if `modinfo` had
#       exited with VENTULUS_GUARD_SIM_MODINFO_RC (default 0, installed); print
#       journal lines to stdout instead of logging them
# Setting any of them requires already being able to control this root
# process's environment. The first two can at worst produce the fail-open
# behaviour that is already the default; the third can at worst load nothing,
# which drives no hardware. They exist so the decision table and the handling
# of modprobe's result are actually tested.
DMI_DIR="${VENTULUS_DMI_DIR:-/sys/class/dmi/id}"
DRY_RUN="${VENTULUS_GUARD_DRY_RUN:-}"
SIM_RC="${VENTULUS_GUARD_SIM_RC:-}"
SIM_STDERR="${VENTULUS_GUARD_SIM_STDERR:-}"
SIM_MODINFO_RC="${VENTULUS_GUARD_SIM_MODINFO_RC:-0}"

MODULE="${1-}"

# No module named: nothing to decide. Fail open by doing nothing rather than
# running modprobe with no arguments.
[ -n "$MODULE" ] || exit 0

# journal <priority> <message>
journal() {
    if [ -n "$SIM_RC" ]; then
        printf 'JOURNAL %s %s\n' "$1" "$2"
        return 0
    fi
    if command -v logger >/dev/null 2>&1 &&
        logger -t ventulus-superio-guard -p "daemon.$1" "$2" 2>/dev/null; then
        return 0
    fi
    printf 'ventulus-superio-guard: %s\n' "$2" >&2
}

# Is the module installed for the running kernel? `modinfo` answers from the
# module index, which the install rule does not touch: it reports "not found"
# for a module the rule names but the kernel lacks. With no modinfo we cannot
# tell, so answer yes and keep modprobe's failure.
module_installed() {
    if [ -n "$SIM_RC" ]; then
        return "$SIM_MODINFO_RC"
    fi
    command -v modinfo >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 0
    modinfo -n -- "$MODULE" >/dev/null 2>&1
}

# `--ignore-install` is what stops this rule re-entering itself.
run_modprobe() {
    if [ -n "$SIM_RC" ]; then
        printf '%s\n' "$SIM_STDERR" >&2
        return "$SIM_RC"
    fi
    modprobe --ignore-install "$@"
}

load_it() {
    if [ -n "$DRY_RUN" ]; then
        printf 'LOAD %s\n' "$MODULE"
        exit 0
    fi
    if [ -z "$SIM_RC" ] && ! command -v modprobe >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        # No modprobe on PATH. That is not "fail open" -- the module does not
        # get loaded -- so say so rather than exiting 0 in silence and leaving
        # a Nuvoton board mysteriously without sensors. Still exit 0: a non-zero
        # exit here would fail the boot-time modprobe for a condition the user
        # cannot act on mid-boot.
        journal err "could not run modprobe to load $MODULE; the module has NOT been loaded"
        exit 0
    fi
    # modprobe's stdout goes straight through; its stderr is captured, so that
    # it can be read, and is then passed on unchanged.
    { err=$(run_modprobe "$@" 2>&1 1>&3 3>&-); rc=$?; } 3>&1
    [ -z "$err" ] || printf '%s\n' "$err" >&2
    [ "$rc" -ne 0 ] || exit 0
    # LC_ALL=C above keeps strerror's text in English. A wording this pattern
    # misses falls back to the non-zero exit below, as before PKG-i. The text
    # must END the line: ENXIO reads "No such device or address", a different
    # failure that keeps its non-zero exit.
    nl='
'
    case "$err" in
        *"could not insert '"*"': No such device" | \
        *"could not insert '"*"': No such device$nl"*)
            journal notice "$MODULE found no chip on this board (No such device), so it is not loaded. That is the expected result on a board without the Super-I/O chip $MODULE drives. The guard reports it as success, so that systemd-modules-load does not fail the boot."
            exit 0 ;;
    esac
    if ! module_installed; then
        journal notice "$MODULE is not installed for this kernel, so it is not loaded. systemd-modules-load ignores a module that is not installed; the guard reports it as success for the same reason."
        exit 0
    fi
    exit "$rc"
}

board_name=$(cat "$DMI_DIR/board_name" 2>/dev/null) || board_name=''
board_vendor=$(cat "$DMI_DIR/board_vendor" 2>/dev/null) || board_vendor=''

# Unreadable DMI (container, exotic firmware) -> we cannot judge -> load.
[ -n "$board_name" ] || load_it "$@"

upper() { printf '%s' "$1" | tr '[:lower:]' '[:upper:]'; }
board_uc=$(upper "$board_name")
vendor_uc=$(upper "$board_vendor")

# ── BEGIN GENERATED BOARD LIST ──
# Parity-pinned against `GIGABYTE_DUAL_CHIP_BOARDS` in daemon/src/hwmon/chip_db.rs
# by `superio_guard_board_list_matches_chip_db` -- edit the Rust table, then this,
# and the test will tell you if they diverge. Matched case-insensitively as
# SUBSTRINGS, exactly as chip_db.rs matches them. Since DEC-424 this list only
# names a board and decides the no-vendor case; a Gigabyte vendor alone suppresses.
DUAL_CHIP_ITE_BOARDS='X870E AORUS MASTER
X870E AORUS PRO
X870E AORUS ELITE X3D
X870E AORUS ELITE WIFI7
X870E AORUS XTREME AI TOP
X870 AORUS ELITE WIFI7
X870 AORUS ELITE WIFI7 ICE
X870 AORUS ELITE X3D
B850 AORUS ELITE X3D
X870E EAGLE WIFI7
X870E EAGLE X3D WIFI7
X870E AERO X3D WOOD
X870E AERO X3D DARK WOOD
X870 AORUS TACHYON ICE
X870E AORUS XTREME X3D AI TOP
X670E AORUS MASTER
X670E AORUS PRO X
X670 AORUS ELITE AX
Z690 AORUS PRO
Z690 AORUS MASTER
Z790 AORUS ELITE AX
Z790 AORUS MASTER
Z790 AORUS XTREME
Z790 AORUS PRO X
Z890 AORUS MASTER
Z890 AORUS ELITE X ICE
Z890 AORUS PRO ICE
Z890 AORUS TACHYON ICE
Z890 AORUS TACHYON DUO X ICE
Z890 AORUS XTREME AI TOP
Z890 AI TOP
Z890 AERO D
W880 AI TOP
Z390 AORUS MASTER
Z390 AORUS PRO
Z390 AORUS ULTRA
Z490 AORUS MASTER
Z390 AORUS XTREME
Z490 AORUS PRO AX
Z490 VISION D
Z490 AORUS ULTRA
Z490 AORUS XTREME
W480 VISION D
X570 AORUS MASTER
X570 AORUS PRO
X570 AORUS PRO WIFI
X570 AORUS ULTRA
X570 AORUS XTREME
X570S AERO G
X570S AORUS MASTER
B550 AORUS MASTER
B550 AORUS PRO
TRX40 AORUS XTREME
TRX40 AORUS MASTER
TRX40 AORUS PRO WIFI
TRX40 DESIGNARE
TRX50 AI TOP
TRX50 AERO D
X470 AORUS ULTRA GAMING
X470 AORUS GAMING 7 WIFI
X470 AORUS GAMING 5 WIFI
B450 AORUS PRO
B450 AORUS PRO-CF
X399 DESIGNARE EX
X399 AORUS PRO
X399 AORUS XTREME
X399 AORUS GAMING 7
B550 VISION D
B850 AI TOP
X870 AORUS STEALTH ICE
X870 AORUS STEALTH
B850 AORUS STEALTH'
# ── END GENERATED BOARD LIST ──

matched=''
# Field-split the list on newlines only, so board names keep their spaces.
IFS='
'
for entry in $DUAL_CHIP_ITE_BOARDS; do
    [ -n "$entry" ] || continue
    case "$board_uc" in
        *"$entry"*) matched="$entry"; break ;;
    esac
done
unset IFS

# The decision (DEC-424):
#   a Gigabyte vendor               -> suppress (every Gigabyte board is ITE)
#   no vendor, and a listed board   -> suppress (the name alone is Gigabyte's)
#   anything else                   -> load
case "$vendor_uc" in
    *GIGABYTE*)
        why="$board_name is a Gigabyte board, and Gigabyte boards use ITE Super-I/O chips${matched:+ (known board: $matched)}" ;;
    '')
        [ -n "$matched" ] || load_it "$@"
        why="the firmware reports no board vendor, but $board_name is a listed Gigabyte board ($matched), whose Super-I/O is ITE" ;;
    *)
        load_it "$@" ;;
esac

# Suppressed. Say so in the journal: an absence from `lsmod` cannot distinguish
# "we suppressed it" from "it failed to load", and those need different advice.
if [ -n "$DRY_RUN" ]; then
    # The same reason the journal line gives, so a dry-run exercises `why`
    # (DEC-424 review: under `set -u` a bad expansion there would abort a boot
    # modprobe after deciding to suppress, and no test would have run it).
    printf 'SUPPRESS %s (%s)\n' "$MODULE" "$why"
    exit 0
fi
journal notice "not loading $MODULE: $why. A Nuvoton/Winbond probe writes a config-mode unlock to 0x2E/0x4E, which can latch an ITE eSPI-to-LPC bridge and hide a chip's fan headers (recovery: stop the trigger, reboot, then power down at the wall if still missing). Nothing is lost: this module cannot drive ITE silicon. To load it anyway, create an EMPTY /etc/modprobe.d/ventulus-superio.conf (a copy of this package's file would keep the guard)."
exit 0
